# Commits that touched every line of a file without changing a line of its meaning.
# `git blame` would otherwise credit those lines to the reformatting rather than to
# the change that wrote them, which is the one thing this repository's history is for.
#
# GitHub reads this file by name and skips these revisions in its blame view with no
# setting anywhere. Locally it takes one command, once per clone:
#
#     git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs
#
# Add a revision here only when it is a pure reformatting — if `git diff -w` over it
# shows real content, it does not belong in this file.

# Declared the line endings (LF everywhere, .gitattributes) and renormalized the 28
# blobs still stored CRLF, 2026-09-21. `git diff -w` over it reports .gitattributes
# and nothing else.
#
# Measured after the fact: this one barely needed ignoring. Blame re-attributes a
# re-ended line to the commit that wrote its content, so the only lines this commit
# still owns are BLANK ones (91 of 91 in SkyDome.cs) — and a blank line is exactly
# what --ignore-revs-file cannot rescue, being indistinguishable from every other
# blank line. Kept for the next reformatting commit, which may not be so lucky.
